1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,418
I built an autonomous agent for Logos
Core.

2
00:00:03,498 --> 00:00:07,790
It runs as a module inside the Logos app,
it holds its own keys,

3
00:00:07,870 --> 00:00:11,537
and it spends its own money on the Logos
Execution Zone —

4
00:00:11,617 --> 00:00:13,722
the public testnet, not a local chain.

5
00:00:13,802 --> 00:00:17,157
Start with the problem, because it is the
only interesting part.

6
00:00:17,237 --> 00:00:20,280
An agent that pays for things needs a
spending limit,

7
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:24,339
and the obvious way to write one is an
if-statement inside the agent.

8
00:00:24,419 --> 00:00:28,398
That is worth nothing: the agent has to
sign without waking anybody up,

9
00:00:28,478 --> 00:00:30,896
so whoever takes the process takes the
key,

10
00:00:30,976 --> 00:00:34,331
and an attacker with the key does not run
your if-statement.

11
00:00:34,411 --> 00:00:36,829
So the limit cannot live in the agent.

12
00:00:36,909 --> 00:00:40,576
It has to live somewhere the agent can
read and cannot write.

13
00:00:40,656 --> 00:00:44,011
On this chain that means an account owned
by a program,

14
00:00:44,091 --> 00:00:45,884
and that is what I built.

15
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,996
This is the repository's own check script,

16
00:00:49,076 --> 00:00:51,408
run from a clean clone against the public
testnet.

17
00:00:51,488 --> 00:00:53,015
Nothing in it is asserted —

18
00:00:53,095 --> 00:00:56,499
every line is computed here or fetched
from the chain while you watch.

19
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,802
The first line is the one the prize asks
for: dev mode is zero.

20
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:14,775
Then the deployment check, which is
arithmetic rather than a lookup.

21
00:01:14,855 --> 00:01:17,216
A deploy transaction hash on this chain

22
00:01:17,296 --> 00:01:20,005
is the hash of the program bytecode
itself.

23
00:01:20,085 --> 00:01:22,446
So the script hashes the binary committed

24
00:01:22,526 --> 00:01:25,236
here and asks the chain for that hash.

25
00:01:25,316 --> 00:01:26,282
If they agree,

26
00:01:26,362 --> 00:01:30,815
the program running on the testnet is
byte-for-byte the program you can read.

27
00:01:30,895 --> 00:01:32,907
There is no registry to trust.

28
00:01:32,987 --> 00:01:37,439
Underneath it, a control: a hash that
cannot exist, which comes back null.

29
00:01:37,519 --> 00:01:42,321
Without that, an RPC that answered yes to
everything would pass just as happily.

30
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,748
This section is the design history,

31
00:01:46,828 --> 00:01:49,490
and I would rather be honest about it than

32
00:01:49,570 --> 00:01:52,537
present the final answer as if I had it
first.

33
00:01:52,617 --> 00:01:55,583
The first two versions put the limits in
the address:

34
00:01:55,663 --> 00:01:59,849
the policy account was a hash of the
owner, the agent and the numbers.

35
00:01:59,929 --> 00:02:02,591
It looks strong — you cannot edit a limit,

36
00:02:02,671 --> 00:02:05,028
because a different limit is a different
address.

37
00:02:05,108 --> 00:02:05,942
It was wrong.

38
00:02:06,022 --> 00:02:10,208
Every combination had an address of its
own and all of them were empty,

39
00:02:10,288 --> 00:02:12,950
so there was always a fresh one to take.

40
00:02:13,030 --> 00:02:15,692
So the third version moved the limits into
the

41
00:02:15,772 --> 00:02:18,738
account's data and gave each agent exactly
one policy account,

42
00:02:18,818 --> 00:02:20,871
derived from the agent and nothing else.

43
00:02:20,951 --> 00:02:22,699
That fixed where a policy goes.

44
00:02:22,779 --> 00:02:25,746
It said nothing about who may put one
there —

45
00:02:25,826 --> 00:02:28,183
and the answer turned out to be anybody.

46
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:34,011
This is the transaction that proves it,
and it is still in a block.

47
00:02:34,091 --> 00:02:36,962
A stranger — an account created for the
purpose,

48
00:02:37,042 --> 00:02:40,570
which has never held the agent's key and
never will —

49
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:43,521
anchored an unlimited policy over one of
my agents.

50
00:02:43,601 --> 00:02:44,833
No key was needed.

51
00:02:44,913 --> 00:02:48,112
It needed the agent's public identifier,
which this repository publishes.

52
00:02:48,192 --> 00:02:50,735
The account that anchor created is still
there,

53
00:02:50,815 --> 00:02:53,031
and it still says what it said:

54
00:02:53,111 --> 00:02:56,966
a stranger owns that agent's only policy,
with no ceiling, for good.

55
00:02:57,046 --> 00:02:59,589
That is not an argument about source code.

56
00:02:59,669 --> 00:03:01,557
It is a matter of record.

57
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,626
The fix is consent, and it is two
signatures in two transactions.

58
00:03:10,706 --> 00:03:14,940
First the agent itself signs, naming the
owner it will accept.

59
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:20,039
The address of that claim is derived from
the account doing the signing,

60
00:03:20,119 --> 00:03:24,353
so there is no identifier in the
instruction to lie about.

61
00:03:24,433 --> 00:03:27,098
Then the designated owner signs the
policy,

62
00:03:27,178 --> 00:03:31,805
and the program reads the claim back and
refuses any other signer.

63
00:03:31,885 --> 00:03:34,158
A third party holds neither key.

64
00:03:34,238 --> 00:03:37,687
It cannot claim an agent it cannot sign
for,

65
00:03:37,767 --> 00:03:42,786
and it cannot anchor over an agent that
has already designated somebody else.

66
00:03:42,866 --> 00:03:48,277
On screen is the identical stranger call,
sent to the program this repository ships.

67
00:03:48,357 --> 00:03:51,022
It was never included in any block.

68
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,809
Three agents, one for each skill category
the prize names: Storage, Messaging,
Blockchain.

69
00:04:18,889 --> 00:04:22,697
Each is a separate deployment on the
public testnet with its own keys.

70
00:04:22,777 --> 00:04:25,688
The first column is the agent's own
shielded account —

71
00:04:25,768 --> 00:04:27,483
that is where its money is.

72
00:04:27,563 --> 00:04:30,175
The second is public, and that asymmetry
is forced:

73
00:04:30,255 --> 00:04:32,269
a payment into a shielded account can

74
00:04:32,349 --> 00:04:34,662
be read back by nobody but its owner,

75
00:04:34,742 --> 00:04:36,756
so an agent that wants to be

76
00:04:36,836 --> 00:04:39,149
paid checkably has to advertise a public
account.

77
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,381
These are the ceilings — a per-transaction
limit, a per-period limit,

78
00:04:51,461 --> 00:04:53,899
and how long a period is in blocks.

79
00:04:53,979 --> 00:04:57,046
I want to be precise about where those
numbers live,

80
00:04:57,126 --> 00:04:58,934
because it is the whole mechanism.

81
00:04:59,014 --> 00:05:00,822
They are not in the agent,

82
00:05:00,902 --> 00:05:03,654
and they are not arguments to the payment
instruction,

83
00:05:03,734 --> 00:05:06,801
which carries an amount and a period and
nothing else.

84
00:05:06,881 --> 00:05:10,891
They are ninety-seven bytes of data in
that account in the middle column,

85
00:05:10,971 --> 00:05:13,409
and the program this repository ships is
the

86
00:05:13,489 --> 00:05:16,241
only thing on the chain that can write
there.

87
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:28,544
Here are those two signatures, on the
public testnet, for all three agents.

88
00:05:28,624 --> 00:05:32,351
The agent's claim first, then the owner's
policy on top of it.

89
00:05:32,431 --> 00:05:36,158
Nothing is anchored on camera, and that is
deliberate rather than convenient.

90
00:05:36,238 --> 00:05:40,282
The claim can only be made once — the
instruction creates the account,

91
00:05:40,362 --> 00:05:44,088
and an instruction that creates an account
refuses one that already exists.

92
00:05:44,168 --> 00:05:46,626
A second attempt has nowhere to be
written.

93
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,585
Use case four, as the prize words it: a
privacy-preserving notary.

94
00:05:58,665 --> 00:06:01,917
The agent timestamps a document, stores it
on Logos Storage,

95
00:06:01,997 --> 00:06:04,249
and records it on the chain —

96
00:06:04,329 --> 00:06:07,248
a proof of existence that is verifiable,
and private.

97
00:06:07,328 --> 00:06:11,579
The trick is what goes on chain, which is
nothing about the document.

98
00:06:11,659 --> 00:06:15,911
The content address the storage node
returns is turned into a signing key.

99
00:06:15,991 --> 00:06:20,575
The chain sees a transaction signed by
that key, and never sees the document,

100
00:06:20,655 --> 00:06:23,241
the address, or even a hash of it.

101
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,341
So anyone holding the document can
rederive the key and check the chain
themselves,

102
00:06:30,421 --> 00:06:33,814
and anyone who does not hold it learns
nothing at all.

103
00:06:33,894 --> 00:06:35,393
That is the whole property.

104
00:06:35,473 --> 00:06:38,551
What you are watching is the re-check, not
the notarisation:

105
00:06:38,631 --> 00:06:41,077
it reads the record back off the chain,

106
00:06:41,157 --> 00:06:43,288
confirms the transaction is in a block,

107
00:06:43,368 --> 00:06:47,077
and confirms it was signed by the key that
this document derives.

108
00:06:47,157 --> 00:06:49,919
Beside it, two controls, both shown red on
purpose.

109
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,304
Use case five: an on-chain event alerter.

110
00:06:57,384 --> 00:07:00,030
The agent watches an account for a state

111
00:07:00,110 --> 00:07:02,755
change and tells its owner over Logos
Messaging.

112
00:07:02,835 --> 00:07:06,161
I want to be careful about what makes this
real,

113
00:07:06,241 --> 00:07:08,205
because it is easy to fake.

114
00:07:08,285 --> 00:07:10,930
The watcher was never told the transaction
hash.

115
00:07:11,010 --> 00:07:14,677
It derived the address it watches from the
agent's own identifier,

116
00:07:14,757 --> 00:07:17,743
noticed the account change, decoded who
had been named,

117
00:07:17,823 --> 00:07:20,127
and then found the transaction that did

118
00:07:20,207 --> 00:07:22,852
it by reading blocks until it found one.

119
00:07:22,932 --> 00:07:24,896
A separate process made the claim.

120
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,296
It is on chain at block eight thousand
nine hundred and forty-five,

121
00:07:31,376 --> 00:07:34,672
and the block either side of it does not
contain that agent.

122
00:07:34,752 --> 00:07:35,797
I checked that separately,

123
00:07:35,877 --> 00:07:39,736
because "found it in a block" is only
evidence if it could have failed.

124
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,287
Now the ceiling itself, from both sides —

125
00:07:55,367 --> 00:07:57,358
not one of the prize's use cases,

126
00:07:57,438 --> 00:07:59,725
but the criterion the other three rest on.

127
00:07:59,805 --> 00:08:02,092
First the script reads the envelope
straight out

128
00:08:02,172 --> 00:08:04,459
of the account on chain and decodes it:

129
00:08:04,539 --> 00:08:06,234
the owner, both limits, the period,

130
00:08:06,314 --> 00:08:08,897
and the total spent so far in this period.

131
00:08:08,977 --> 00:08:11,855
Then it checks those against the manifest
in the repository.

132
00:08:11,935 --> 00:08:15,110
The account is the authority; the file is
only a copy,

133
00:08:15,190 --> 00:08:18,069
and this is the line that catches the copy
drifting.

134
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:24,397
Then it derives the policy account's
address from the agent's identifier,

135
00:08:24,477 --> 00:08:27,289
using the seeds declared in the interface
definition,

136
00:08:27,369 --> 00:08:31,989
and shows there is exactly one derivation
and no limit among the seeds.

137
00:08:32,069 --> 00:08:35,604
That is the demonstration — not that ten
derivations agree,

138
00:08:35,684 --> 00:08:37,412
but that only one exists.

139
00:08:37,492 --> 00:08:40,666
Beside it, a control: an agent nobody ever
anchored,

140
00:08:40,746 --> 00:08:44,281
whose address is real arithmetic and whose
account is empty.

141
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:01,588
Below the ceiling: settlements that
already landed on the public testnet,

142
00:09:01,668 --> 00:09:05,756
each one under the limit, each one
confirmed against the chain,

143
00:09:05,836 --> 00:09:09,924
and each one checked by the recipient's
balance actually moving —

144
00:09:10,004 --> 00:09:11,818
not by the transaction existing.

145
00:09:11,898 --> 00:09:17,123
Above the ceiling: the agent asks for more
than it is allowed, twice —

146
00:09:17,203 --> 00:09:18,638
once for too much,

147
00:09:18,718 --> 00:09:23,564
and once by trying to slide its period
forward to reset the total.

148
00:09:23,644 --> 00:09:26,595
Both refused, and notice how fast that is.

149
00:09:26,675 --> 00:09:31,142
The guest stops at the check, so no
transaction is ever built:

150
00:09:31,222 --> 00:09:34,552
there is nothing to submit and nothing to
reject.

151
00:09:34,632 --> 00:09:37,584
And each refusal carries a named error
code,

152
00:09:37,664 --> 00:09:41,373
because "some error happened" is also what
a typo produces.

153
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,468
Two things I did not demonstrate on the
testnet,

154
00:09:52,548 --> 00:09:56,409
and I would rather say them than have them
found.

155
00:09:56,489 --> 00:09:59,957
An over-threshold payment is not
forbidden, it is routed:

156
00:10:00,037 --> 00:10:04,293
a second instruction takes an approval
only the owner can create,

157
00:10:04,373 --> 00:10:06,658
and that approval carries an expiry,

158
00:10:06,738 --> 00:10:10,600
because an approval that never expires is
a bearer instrument.

159
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:14,147
A third lets the owner change the limits
later,

160
00:10:14,227 --> 00:10:16,907
carrying the spent total through untouched
—

161
00:10:16,987 --> 00:10:19,666
raising a ceiling is not forgiveness for

162
00:10:19,746 --> 00:10:22,819
what was already spent under the old one.

163
00:10:22,899 --> 00:10:27,944
Both are exercised against the deployed
binary; neither has landed on the testnet.

164
00:10:28,024 --> 00:10:31,097
The reason is written down rather than
hidden:

165
00:10:31,177 --> 00:10:33,856
every transaction is built against nonce
zero,

166
00:10:33,936 --> 00:10:37,404
so a fresh public account gets one program
transaction,

167
00:10:37,484 --> 00:10:39,769
and these owners spent theirs anchoring.

168
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:44,365
Everything in this film ran against the
public LEZ testnet.

169
00:10:44,445 --> 00:10:47,898
There is no local sequencer anywhere in
it, the proofs are real,

170
00:10:47,978 --> 00:10:51,137
and every hash on screen can be looked up
by anyone.

171
00:10:56,200 --> 00:10:59,041
This is use case two: an agent services
marketplace.

172
00:10:59,121 --> 00:11:01,963
One agent advertises a priced skill,
another discovers it,

173
00:11:02,043 --> 00:11:05,533
has the work done, and pays for it, with
nobody approving.

174
00:11:05,613 --> 00:11:09,753
What makes that autonomous is not that no
human happened to be watching.

175
00:11:09,833 --> 00:11:12,025
It is that the chain would have

176
00:11:12,105 --> 00:11:14,621
refused the payment if it had needed one.

177
00:11:14,701 --> 00:11:18,192
One warning, because it is the whole
middle of this film:

178
00:11:18,272 --> 00:11:19,815
the proof takes several minutes,

179
00:11:19,895 --> 00:11:23,710
and for most of it the only thing moving
is a heartbeat.

180
00:11:23,790 --> 00:11:25,333
That is not a hang.

181
00:11:25,413 --> 00:11:28,579
That is what a real proof costs on a
laptop.

182
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:36,158
The storage agent publishes an A2A Agent
Card.

183
00:11:36,238 --> 00:11:40,599
A2A gives me the vocabulary — the card,
the skill, the task lifecycle —

184
00:11:40,679 --> 00:11:43,771
and deliberately leaves out the two things
I need most:

185
00:11:43,851 --> 00:11:46,309
payment, and any way to trust the card.

186
00:11:46,389 --> 00:11:47,895
So the card is signed,

187
00:11:47,975 --> 00:11:52,019
by the key that owns the account the card
asks you to pay.

188
00:11:52,099 --> 00:11:56,143
An unsigned card is a document anyone on
the discovery topic can rewrite,

189
00:11:56,223 --> 00:11:57,095
payment account included,

190
00:11:57,175 --> 00:11:59,633
and "discover an agent and pay it" would

191
00:11:59,713 --> 00:12:02,171
then mean "pay whoever edited the card
last".

192
00:12:04,700 --> 00:12:07,456
The client verifies that signature with a
separate verifier,

193
00:12:07,536 --> 00:12:09,347
not with the signing code —

194
00:12:09,427 --> 00:12:12,814
two implementations of the same curve
arithmetic is how a verifier

195
00:12:12,894 --> 00:12:16,596
ends up agreeing with a signer about a
signature that is wrong.

196
00:12:16,676 --> 00:12:19,747
Then it compares the price against its own
anchored ceiling.

197
00:12:19,827 --> 00:12:24,160
Inside the envelope, so it takes the
autonomous branch and no owner is asked.

198
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:38,314
The task lifecycle: submitted, working,
completed.

199
00:12:38,394 --> 00:12:42,702
Then the agent re-syncs its private
account before it proves anything —

200
00:12:42,782 --> 00:12:47,090
the proof is built against its local view
of its own notes,

201
00:12:47,170 --> 00:12:51,844
and a stale view produces a valid-looking
hash the chain will never accept.

202
00:13:06,700 --> 00:13:09,723
Now the settlement, and this is the line
that matters:

203
00:13:09,803 --> 00:13:12,827
it signs from the agent's own wallet, not
the owner's.

204
00:13:12,907 --> 00:13:14,689
That is what unattended means here.

205
00:13:14,769 --> 00:13:17,482
Signing this with the owner's key would
prove nothing.

206
00:13:17,562 --> 00:13:19,033
And now the prover starts.

207
00:13:19,113 --> 00:13:20,275
Dev mode is zero,

208
00:13:20,355 --> 00:13:22,447
so this is a real proof of

209
00:13:22,527 --> 00:13:24,930
two programs and the call between them —

210
00:13:25,010 --> 00:13:28,964
the policy program checking the ceiling,
and the transfer program moving the
balance.

211
00:13:43,200 --> 00:13:44,928
The heartbeat is the prover.

212
00:13:45,008 --> 00:13:49,630
That percentage is r0vm, the RISC-Zero
virtual machine, measured off the process
table,

213
00:13:49,710 --> 00:13:54,694
and that is what it looks like with every
core on this machine busy.

214
00:13:54,774 --> 00:13:56,864
Dev mode cannot produce that line.

215
00:13:56,944 --> 00:13:59,395
It returns a fake receipt in milliseconds

216
00:13:59,475 --> 00:14:02,289
and never starts a prover at all —

217
00:14:02,369 --> 00:14:06,629
so the line is itself the evidence that
the proof is real,

218
00:14:06,709 --> 00:14:10,246
and it carries the dev mode setting on
every repeat.

219
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:26,874
The lines above it are still on screen and
worth reading.

220
00:14:26,954 --> 00:14:27,556
The nonce,

221
00:14:27,636 --> 00:14:32,333
which makes this payment distinct from any
other with the same recipient and amount.

222
00:14:32,413 --> 00:14:37,111
The block the chain was at, and the period
this spend is accounted to.

223
00:14:37,191 --> 00:14:41,206
And the recipient's balance, read off the
chain before anything was signed.

224
00:14:41,286 --> 00:14:44,959
An earlier version of this instruction
produced a confirmed on-chain proof

225
00:14:45,039 --> 00:14:48,713
that a policy permitted a payment and
moved nothing at all.

226
00:14:48,793 --> 00:14:51,102
It was written up as a settlement.

227
00:14:51,182 --> 00:14:53,832
That is why the balance is read twice.

228
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:17,718
While we wait, the architecture, because
this is the part a diagram would flatten.

229
00:15:17,798 --> 00:15:20,016
The module is a Logos Core plugin.

230
00:15:20,096 --> 00:15:24,285
It exposes three skill categories to the
app — Storage, Messaging, Blockchain —

231
00:15:24,365 --> 00:15:26,584
and each is a thin binding onto

232
00:15:26,664 --> 00:15:29,211
a real Logos node or onto this chain.

233
00:15:29,291 --> 00:15:31,182
The agent logic sits above them.

234
00:15:31,262 --> 00:15:34,794
The spending policy does not sit in the
module at all.

235
00:15:34,874 --> 00:15:39,392
It is a program deployed on the testnet,
and the module cannot alter it.

236
00:15:39,472 --> 00:15:43,004
If somebody replaced the module tomorrow,
the ceiling would still hold.

237
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:20,284
The other decision worth stating is the
one that took four deployments to find.

238
00:16:20,364 --> 00:16:23,972
I kept trying to make the anchoring call
honest —

239
00:16:24,052 --> 00:16:27,292
bind the identifiers, compare the limits,
check the caller.

240
00:16:27,372 --> 00:16:30,980
Each version added a comparison, and each
was walked around,

241
00:16:31,060 --> 00:16:33,193
because the attacker was never lying.

242
00:16:33,273 --> 00:16:36,881
They really were the owner of the policy
they anchored.

243
00:16:36,961 --> 00:16:40,570
They had simply never been asked whether
the agent agreed.

244
00:16:40,650 --> 00:16:43,152
The answer was not a better comparison.

245
00:16:43,232 --> 00:16:47,947
It was to ask the agent, once, in a
transaction it signs itself.

246
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:27,360
Two things this film does not show,

247
00:17:27,440 --> 00:17:30,560
and I would rather say them than have them
found.

248
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:33,120
No language model runs behind the
inference skill.

249
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:36,000
The port is real and both backends are
written,

250
00:17:36,080 --> 00:17:39,200
but one is a stub with an honest name and

251
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:42,399
the other has never had a request leave
this repository.

252
00:17:42,479 --> 00:17:46,239
The price decision you saw was a shell
comparison, not a model.

253
00:17:46,319 --> 00:17:49,439
And in the file-vault case the encryption
is the script's,

254
00:17:49,519 --> 00:17:53,599
not the module's — the storage skill is a
passthrough to the node.

255
00:17:53,679 --> 00:17:57,119
That is a real gap, written down rather
than papered over.

256
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:49,071
Still proving, and nothing new will print
until it finishes,

257
00:18:49,151 --> 00:18:53,418
because the script hands the prover's
output back all at once.

258
00:18:53,498 --> 00:18:56,183
Which is worth a sentence about privacy.

259
00:18:56,263 --> 00:19:00,530
This is a privacy-preserving transaction,
so the payer stays shielded —

260
00:19:00,610 --> 00:19:03,691
nobody watching the chain learns which
agent paid.

261
00:19:03,771 --> 00:19:09,222
The payee is public, and that is
deliberately the only checkable side of
it.

262
00:19:09,302 --> 00:19:12,383
Privacy for the spender, accountability
for the recipient.

263
00:19:46,200 --> 00:19:50,105
When it finishes, one line appears: the
settlement transaction hash.

264
00:19:50,185 --> 00:19:53,292
Then the script stops proving and starts
waiting,

265
00:19:53,372 --> 00:19:56,480
because a submitted hash is not a payment.

266
00:19:56,560 --> 00:19:59,269
Blocks here are about a minute apart.

267
00:20:03,200 --> 00:20:04,117
There it is.

268
00:20:04,197 --> 00:20:06,776
Signed by the agent, on the public
testnet.

269
00:20:06,856 --> 00:20:09,435
Now it polls for inclusion and refuses to

270
00:20:09,515 --> 00:20:12,094
write anything down if the transaction
never lands.

271
00:20:12,174 --> 00:20:14,753
A hash that was submitted and a hash

272
00:20:14,833 --> 00:20:17,745
that was dropped look identical to the RPC
—

273
00:20:17,825 --> 00:20:19,407
both come back null —

274
00:20:19,487 --> 00:20:23,395
so absence proves nothing and the script
waits properly rather than guessing.

275
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:45,533
Landed.

276
00:20:45,553 --> 00:20:50,412
And now the check that matters: the
recipient's balance, read off the chain
again.

277
00:20:50,492 --> 00:20:55,351
The difference is the price, exactly, and
no owner signed anything at any point.

278
00:20:55,431 --> 00:20:57,821
Only now does it write the manifest.

279
00:20:57,901 --> 00:21:02,407
On any other outcome it refuses to, and
leaves the previous record alone.

280
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:07,820
And then every settlement the marketplace
has ever produced is

281
00:21:07,900 --> 00:21:10,791
decoded out of the chain's own copy of the
transaction —

282
00:21:10,871 --> 00:21:12,951
not out of the file that recorded it.

283
00:21:13,031 --> 00:21:15,111
The running total in the policy account is

284
00:21:15,191 --> 00:21:17,272
the sum of every price charged to it.

285
00:21:17,352 --> 00:21:19,162
That number is not kept by the

286
00:21:19,242 --> 00:21:21,322
agent and not passed in by the caller.

287
00:21:21,402 --> 00:21:24,023
It lives in the same ninety-seven bytes as
the limits,

288
00:21:24,103 --> 00:21:25,913
and the period it belongs to is

289
00:21:25,993 --> 00:21:28,073
pinned to the transaction's own block
validity window.

290
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:32,647
That is the whole thing.

291
00:21:32,727 --> 00:21:34,480
An agent discovered a priced service,

292
00:21:34,560 --> 00:21:38,452
decided by itself that it could afford it,
proved it was allowed to,

293
00:21:38,532 --> 00:21:41,812
and paid — on a public chain, with a real
proof,

294
00:21:41,892 --> 00:21:44,562
against a ceiling it has no way to raise.

295
00:21:44,642 --> 00:21:46,700
The last line is an explorer link.

296
00:21:46,780 --> 00:21:50,061
The explorer indexes about an hour and
three-quarters behind the sequencer,

297
00:21:50,141 --> 00:21:52,199
so it may still say not found.

298
00:21:52,279 --> 00:21:54,338
The RPC has already returned this
transaction.
